---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { hypothesis } from '../data/project';
---

<Layout
  title="Research"
  description="Exploring tone, phonemes, dialect and native-speaker evaluation for more natural Igbo speech technology."
  ><PageHero
    eyebrow="RESEARCH / PLANNING & RESEARCH"
    title="Taking the language seriously. Testing the possibilities."
    description="Our focus is native-quality pronunciation: tone, phonetics, dialect and the evidence needed to know when a system is improving."
  /><section class="section container two-col">
    <aside>
      <span class="eyebrow">THE CHALLENGE</span><h2>
        More than<br />reading letters.
      </h2><div class="role-list">
        <span>Tone</span><span>Diacritics</span><span>Dialect</span><span
          >Context</span
        ><span>Prosody</span>
      </div>
    </aside><div class="prose">
      <p class="lead">For a tonal language, sound is part of meaning.</p><p>
        A speech system may recognise the letters in an Igbo word and still
        pronounce it incorrectly. Tone, vowel distinctions, context and rhythm
        all affect how an utterance is heard. Personal names, place names and
        code-switching create further challenges.
      </p><p>
        Dialect diversity also matters. Evaluation should respect different
        legitimate ways of speaking Igbo, rather than flattening them into one
        artificial standard.
      </p><p>
        Limited high-quality, appropriately licensed speech data can make
        progress harder. Our approach starts with data provenance, linguistic
        annotation and the people who speak the language.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section><section class="soft-section section" id="hypothesis">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">THE PRIMARY RESEARCH QUESTION</span><h2
        style="margin-top:0"
      >
        {hypothesis}
      </h2><p>
        This is a hypothesis to investigate, not a performance claim. We plan to
        compare documented baselines using both automated measures and
        native-speaker evaluation.
      </p><h3>How we intend to test it</h3><ol>
        <li>
          Review existing data, models, licences and evaluation methods.
        </li><li>
          Design consented, linguistically annotated evaluation material.
        </li><li>
          Establish character-based and general-purpose baselines.
        </li><li>Compare explicit tone, phoneme and dialect supervision.</li><li
        >
          Use blind native-speaker comparisons, report uncertainty and publish
          limitations.
        </li>
      </ol><p>
        We aim to connect these capabilities in reusable infrastructure. The
        landscape review will determine where to extend existing work and where
        new research is warranted.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section><section class="section container" id="method">
    <div class="two-col"><div>
      <span class="eyebrow">A CONTROLLED COMPARISON</span><h2>Measure the linguistic choices.</h2>
    </div><div class="prose">
      <p>We will compare plain orthographic input, reviewed tone-marked input and phoneme-plus-tone input using matched audio, data splits, model family and training conditions.</p>
      <p>Manually reviewed gold annotations and automatically predicted annotations will be evaluated separately. Automatic tone restoration is useful only when downstream tests show that it helps without introducing meaning-changing errors.</p>
      <p>ASR round-trip scores alone cannot establish TTS quality. A system can transcribe its own output while still sounding unnatural, changing tone or losing a meaning contrast.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="panel" style="margin-top:32px"><span class="eyebrow">BENCHMARK-FIRST SEQUENCE</span><ol>
      <li>Review data rights, provenance and permissions.</li><li>Reproduce named baselines before changing the data.</li><li>Use a held-out challenge set and sealed future-audit material.</li><li>Run controlled experiments and blind native-speaker listening.</li><li>Report errors, uncertainty, operating latency and cost.</li><li>Publish limitations before expanding scope.</li>
    </ol><p><strong>Evaluation dimensions:</strong> pronunciation and tone errors, meaning confusions, human transcription accuracy, naturalness and preference, omissions and repetitions, dialect-group results, WER/CER where relevant, latency and operating cost.</p><p>Exact release thresholds will be preregistered after baseline measurement and before final testing.</p></div>
  </section><section class="section container" id="landscape">
    <span class="eyebrow">THE EXISTING LANDSCAPE</span><h2>
      Important work already exists.
    </h2><p style="max-width:730px;margin-top:20px">
      These resources and communities inform the research landscape. Inclusion
      does not imply a partnership, endorsement, data access or permission to
      reuse material. Coverage and licences must be checked per resource.
    </p><div class="panel">
      <h3>The initial landscape review is complete.</h3><p>
        Our living register brings together speech data, language models,
        pronunciation resources and access considerations. Phase 0 continues
        with collaborator mapping, provenance auditing and consultation.
      </p><a class="button" href="/research/landscape"
        >Explore the Igbo AI Landscape →</a
      >
    </div>
  </section></Layout
>
